Lectin Sensitivity
Integrative & Functional medicine
Severe GI distress following legume/nightshade consumption, Widespread migrating joint aching, Brain fog correlating with dietary intake, Resolution on low-lectin diet

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-021
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unbearable abdominal cramping, intractable diarrhea, and severe joint stiffness requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute inflammatory bowel disease flare or acute infectious colitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden, severe abdominal spasms, profuse diarrhea, and severe systemic inflammation (joint stiffness) following a specific meal. Urgent on-site physical and abdominal assessment was strictly required to exclude acute surgical abdomen, severe infectious gastroenteritis, or severe dehydration, preventing profound metabolic derangement prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping so violently I can't stand up, my joints are completely locked, and I can barely keep a thought in my head."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a rapid onset of severe, sudden, and distinct intense gastrointestinal distress beginning 3 hours after the consumption of a large meal containing specific nightshades (eggplant, tomatoes) and legumes (lentils). The patient describes chronic, severe abdominal cramping, intense bloating, and distinctly altered bowel habits, currently manifesting as profuse, painful diarrhea. The patient also complains of severe brain fog, prominent distinct cognitive sluggishness, and significantly poor concentration directly correlating with this acute gastrointestinal flare-up. Furthermore, the patient is experiencing widespread vague migrating joint aching and distinct profound severe muscle stiffness that has exacerbated an underlying chronic autoimmune condition (rheumatoid arthritis). The patient notes profound generalized fatigue and systemic lethargy. Historically, the patient notes distinct clinical improvement upon initiating a highly strict distinctly low-lectin elimination diet, but accidentally consumed triggers tonight.
Functional Status: Bedbound, frequently rushing to the bathroom, unable to physically straighten joints due to pain and stiffness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of acute dehydration and fall risk due to urgent bathroom trips.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, guarding the abdomen, and noticeably stiff when attempting to move.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals diffuse, moderate-to-severe tenderness and prominent generalized bloating, with highly hyperactive bowel sounds. Crucially, there is no true rigidity, involuntary guarding, or rebound tenderness indicating peritonitis. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates widespread, profound severe muscle stiffness and migrating joint aching without focal, hot monoarticular effusion, though small joints of the hands appear chronically mildly deformed (consistent with RA history). Neurological exam is grossly intact, but processing speed is remarkably slow (brain fog). The clinical picture perfectly aligns with the tetrad of severe GI distress following legume/nightshade consumption, widespread migrating joint aching, and brain fog correlating with dietary intake. Confirmation relies on the resolution of symptoms on a low-lectin diet.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen shows remains of the offending meal. Bathroom shows signs of frequent use.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter NSAIDs for joint pain (currently ineffective); no current DMARDs or biologics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lectin Sensitivity (Dietary Flare) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Infectious Gastroenteritis (e.g., Salmonella/Campylobacter) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, bloody diarrhea, and the precise temporal correlation with specific known dietary triggers (lectins) rather than generalized food poisoning.

Acute Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of gross hematochezia or chronic weight loss, though formal endoscopy/calprotectin would be required for absolute exclusion.

Acute Surgical Abdomen (e.g., Appendicitis/Perforation) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft abdomen lacking localized peritonitis, rigidity, or rebound tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal cramping, profuse diarrhea, and widespread systemic inflammation. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need to evaluate for a potential acute surgical abdomen, severe infectious colitis, or impending severe dehydration. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to auscultate bowel sounds, perform deep abdominal palpation to check for peritonism, and evaluate hydration status. The physical exclusion of a rigid abdomen and the confirmation of hyperactive bowel sounds confirmed the absence of an immediate surgical emergency. However, the severity of the painful abdominal spasms, the inability to retain oral fluids, and the debilitating joint stiffness dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of severe dehydration, uncontrolled pain, and extreme physical distress. This urgent evaluation unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Loperamide 4mg stat to slow gut motility and reduce fluid loss. Advised strict bowel rest (clear fluids only) for 12 hours. Prescribed a short course of Paracetamol 1g QID PRN for joint analgesia (avoiding NSAIDs to protect gut lining). Recommend routine GP follow-up for autoimmune monitoring and dietary support.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute gut permeability and systemic inflammation, initiate specific dietary and hydration protocols. Action: Consume 500ml of an oral rehydration solution (electrolyte fluid) slowly over the next 2 hours. Consequence: Targeted electrolyte replacement directly restores intravascular volume lost to diarrhea, explicitly rewarding the patient with the cessation of orthostatic dizziness and tachycardia. Action: Implement a strict 48-hour "bland, low-lectin" reset diet (e.g., plain white rice, steamed low-lectin vegetables, lean protein). Consequence: Removing the specific protein triggers physically halts the immune cross-reactivity in the gut and joints, directly rewarding the patient with a clearance of the severe brain fog and migrating joint pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours to assess hydration and joint status.

